WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.670
For nearly a century
Kodak completely owned

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.290
the nostalgia market.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.336
Oh yeah!

00:00:08.336 --> 00:00:11.485
♪ Got so much style with
that silly old smile ♪

00:00:11.485 --> 00:00:15.077
♪ I'm gonna get you with a Kodak kiss ♪

00:00:15.077 --> 00:00:18.580
Any memorable moment from
a child's first steps

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.673
to a wedding, was branded a Kodak moment.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.210
But recently a Kodak moment
has become, let's say

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:28.650
less nostalgic.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.960
At some point, I think
became a crypto company.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.630
And now apparently it's
a pharmaceutical company.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.870
In 2020, the American film
giant has made headlines

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.860
for it's foray into, of all
things, pharmaceuticals.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.990
Specifically to treat
COVID-19, with the help of

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:47.923
the $765 million U.S. government loan.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:52.310
And if you think that's kind
of odd, you're not alone.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.850
Just so many weird themes of
America today, I think are

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.007
wrapped into this story which
is why it's so fascinating.

00:00:57.007 --> 00:01:01.570
[MSNBC News Announcer]
$765 million loan for Kodak

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:04.120
to begin producing pharmaceuticals.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.010
Turn another, it's a Trump story.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.000
They're doing something
that's a different field.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.260
And it's a field that
they've really hired some of

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:11.600
the best people in the world.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:12.940
Governor Cuomo's involved.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.550
It will create 300 new jobs.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.060
And the story of how
Kodak is trying to become

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.910
a pharmaceutical company
is just as strange as

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.543
the announcement itself.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.730
This is how Kodak went
from selling nostalgia

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.243
to making drugs.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.510
Kodak is a iconic American company.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:45.510
It once symbolized everything
that was great about America.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:47.620
For the better part of a
century, Kodak could be

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.190
considered the closest
thing to a monopoly without

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.410
ever becoming one.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:52.963
And that was no accident.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.400
Founded in 1888 by George
Eastman, Kodak was born

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.794
with one mission, to
revolutionize the world

00:01:59.794 --> 00:02:01.240
of picture taking.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.580
He wanted the camera to be
as ubiquitous and as easy

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.330
to use as the pencil.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:10.310
And he adopted a business
strategy that says

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.370
to the customer, "You push the
button and we do the rest".

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:15.920
The rest would turn out to be

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.733
an extremely aggressive business strategy.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.385
Look at what happened in
the first 40 or 50 years

00:02:22.385 --> 00:02:24.230
of the 20th century.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.830
Scale was the way to win in this.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.390
There were a lot of little
camera companies and a lot of

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:31.500
photo companies and photofinishing
companies that sprang up.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.000
It got into the camera business
by buying a whole bunch

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.500
of camera companies that not
always wanted to get bought.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.200
Typically that doesn't happen
by just soft management.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.690
That happens, pretty tough management.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.950
By acquiring these
companies Kodak was able

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:49.710
to not only dominate the camera
market but the film market

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.120
as well.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.770
One of the reasons it
became so powerful is

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.950
that Eastman understood either
sell the cameras cheaply

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.252
or give them away because
we're gonna make all our money

00:02:58.252 --> 00:03:00.990
on film and photo processing.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:02.703
And it worked on so many levels.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.810
Everything was vertically integrated.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:09.340
You bought your Kodak camera
where you put Kodak film inside

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.210
and-

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.800
Well if you brought your film in to get it

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.480
developed, you brought
it to a Kodak store.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.400
Throughout much of the 20th century Kodak

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.750
could do no wrong.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.480
Every product launch from
cameras like the Brownie

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.390
and the Instamatic, to
engineering feats in film

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.320
like Kodachrome and Ektachrome were a hit.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.305
Even creeping into pop culture.

00:03:28.305 --> 00:03:32.805
♪ Momma don't take my Kodachrome away ♪

00:03:35.928 --> 00:03:37.450
Kodak didn't just set
the tone for the film

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.193
and camera industry, it was
the film and camera industry.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.520
In the mid-70s, 1976 Kodak owned 95%

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:52.160
of the film market and
85% of the camera market

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.590
in the United States.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:56.850
Employment peaked in the
eighties at like 150,000.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.330
This wasn't just a fortune
500 company, this was

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:00.860
a fortune 50 company.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.760
It was a brand that everybody recognized

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.890
and it was dominant.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.800
But Kodak didn't just do
photography because of

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.460
the chemicals used in
film, they also developed

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:14.350
blood analyzers, x-rays,
pharmaceutical manufacturing

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.223
and copiers, just to name a few.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.230
What Kodak didn't know at
the time was an invention

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:24.700
by one of its own engineers
in 1976, would eventually lead

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.540
to the organization's demise.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.820
Kodak engineer Steven Sasson,
invented the prototype

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:33.253
that would eventually
become the digital camera.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.910
It was a feasibility demonstration.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.830
You saw pictures of it
and it was obviously not

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.100
a commercial product.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.580
Kodak saw the writing on
the wall that their days,

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:46.360
while still decades away
were ultimately numbered

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.100
as a film business.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.160
I began to look at
something Gordon Moore had

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.670
come out with just a
couple of years before that

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.250
and it became known as Moore's Law.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:02.430
Moore's Law said that the
capacity of transistors on

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:06.120
an integrated circuit was
gonna double every 18 months.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:11.047
We were able to conclude
in 1978, that by about 2008

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.980
consumer electronic
imaging oughtta overtake

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.830
traditional photography.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:18.870
At the time Kodak wasn't
worried it had a leg up

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:22.440
on the competition and had
plenty of time to prepare.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.590
Dealing with major shifts in
technology that quickly dated

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.470
their research, the
transition to digital signaled

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.680
the beginning of Kodak's decline.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.590
During the early 80s
recognizing this transformation

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:37.090
was coming, Kodak set up an
internal school where we sent

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:40.370
chemists and chemical
engineers to in-house school

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:42.320
to turn them into electrical engineers.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.560
That turned out to be not very successful.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.370
If you just look at the earning
statement of the company

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:51.340
you'd have to say, "What
are we doing this for?

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.170
Because at this point
they're still making money

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:54.153
hand over fist.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.470
There was kind of an ingrained fear

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.900
of cannibalizing that
core business, of killing

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.190
the golden goose.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:03.530
They were a monopoly in
film but they couldn't stop

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:04.890
disruptive technologies.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:08.240
You know, from coming along
and unseating their business.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:12.370
It's sort of the classic
innovator's dilemma problem.

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:16.110
If you have this great cash
cow which is your film camera

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:19.170
business and you're one of
the dominant players there

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:23.400
you're reluctant to totally
disrupt it yourself.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.960
By the time the 90s rolled
around, Kodak's push

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.260
into digital was in full force.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:32.970
CEO Georg Fischer, who headed
Kodak from 1993 to 1999,

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:36.030
pumped $2 billion into
research and development

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:37.570
for digital technology.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:40.243
They had this event in San Francisco.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.453
It was March 28th, 1995.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.430
They call it the big event.

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:48.530
The Chicago Tribune, I remember
they had their headline

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:49.920
was "The Big Gamble".

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.330
And it was Fischer's big
announcement that you know,

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:57.823
Kodak was moving you know,
full steam ahead to digital.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.360
And each attempt was a flop whether it was

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:05.530
the massively overpriced DCS
100, the photo CD that could

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:08.310
hold up to 25 images,
which was quickly outpaced

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.530
by the growing capacity of hard drives.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:13.420
And the DC 20, an incredibly cheap camera

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:16.233
that everyone agreed took awful pictures.

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:19.600
The disc camera was a disaster.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.280
They called it the
adventure of the incredibly

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:22.430
shrinking negative.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:26.060
And it was so small that
the images were horrible.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:28.220
The 90s were also a time when Kodak

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:30.270
started spinning off its other businesses

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:32.120
to pay down its debt.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:35.180
Selling its entire office
imaging business, its digital

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.380
printer and copier division and turning

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.320
its chemical division
into Eastman Chemical.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.200
In 2003, under new CEO
Antonio Pérez, who joined

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.460
from Hewlett Packard, the
company once again made the claim

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.300
that Kodak would become the
leader in digital cameras.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.770
Kodak is back!

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:54.250
They're taking this digital
thing to a level on dreamed of.

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:58.516
They learned early on that
the devices weren't the thing.

00:07:58.516 --> 00:08:01.050
It was what the devices allowed you to do.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.450
It was the fall on business,
all the ancillary stuff

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:04.930
is where they made the money.

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:08.800
They didn't have the right
kinda culture to sell cameras,

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.410
digital cameras in a frenetic
you know, low barrier to entry

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:16.290
market and to beat back everybody else.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:18.540
Compared with cameras that require film

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.910
margins related to the
use of digital cameras

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:22.470
are a disaster.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:25.360
Film generates a profit
of as much as 70 cents

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:26.410
on the dollar.

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:29.100
Digital imaging might make a nickel.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.910
It's like that old joke
about like we're losing

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:34.090
money on every unit we make
but we'll make it up on volume.

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:38.490
In 1987, the company
employed 145,000 people

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:41.580
at its enormous facility in Rochester.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:45.440
20 years later in 2020, that
number has fallen all the way

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.403
down to 4,900 and it keeps on shrinking.

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:53.610
I would like to take this
time to answer a few important

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:55.440
questions about Kodak.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.140
In light of our decision
to file for reorganization.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:00.980
With the failure to successfully pivot

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:05.580
to digital complete, on
January 19th, 2012 Kodak filed

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:06.780
for bankruptcy.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:09.480
This once great company
files for bankruptcy

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.150
and now they're trying to
build with whatever you can.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:14.570
So you get Jim Continenza
comes in as the chairman at

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:17.610
that point, is sort of
their standard barer.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.810
While this was the end of Kodak as mainly

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:23.850
a film business Kodak emerged
from bankruptcy in 2013

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:26.690
and started the first of its many pivots.

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:29.560
Well, some investors are
crazy about cryptocurrencies

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:30.393
right now.

00:09:30.393 --> 00:09:34.420
Kodak is launching its own
cryptocurrency KodakCoin.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.650
The cryptocurrency system
will allow photographers

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:40.830
to register work that
they can license and then

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:42.280
receive payment.

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.530
It was coming at a time where
it seemed like everybody

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:47.460
was announcing a coin as a
way to raise a bunch of money

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:49.040
because there was so much mania.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:52.490
And so you have Kodak, which
is a publicly traded company.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:55.100
A lot of the crypto coin ideas
didn't have a public company

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:56.320
to latch on to.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:59.070
So you attach this idea to
a public company and all of

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.520
a sudden, the stock price soars.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:05.330
In one day of trading shares soared 120%.

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:10.330
Going from $3.10 to $10,
but it wouldn't last.

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:13.643
It was widely lampooned
I think by the press.

00:10:13.643 --> 00:10:16.210
I think the BBC called
it one of the worst ideas

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:19.640
to come out of CS or something
like that at the time.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.883
It was during legit crypto mania.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:25.060
From the peak of the
announcement of KodakCoin

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:30.060
until early 2020, Kodak's stock
price fell from $36.88 cents

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:33.270
to around $2.70 cents.

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:34.210
Until-

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:37.530
Shares of the company soared
yesterday after news that

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:42.340
the U.S. government awarded
the firm $765 million in loans.

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:44.070
They just need to become
a cannabis company

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:46.520
and a meat-free company
and then they've really got

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:48.281
the trifecta of all things,

00:10:48.281 --> 00:10:50.613
millennial and gen Z.
Don't forget electric cars.

00:10:50.613 --> 00:10:52.670
Most people heard about the Kodak loan

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:54.930
on you know, July 28th.

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:56.813
We have president Trump
himself talking about

00:10:56.813 --> 00:11:00.300
the $765 million loan,
we have Peter Navarro,

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.320
the trade representative,
going on TV talking about it

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.600
and the governor of New
York, Andrew Cuomo, thanking

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:07.760
the president for it.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.900
That all of a sudden wakes
everybody up in the world

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:10.733
to this.

00:11:10.733 --> 00:11:13.270
And just like with the
announcement of KodakCoin-

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:17.250
The share price goes crazy,
it tops out at $60 when it had

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:20.750
been trading around $3
for months and months.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:23.227
For a moment, it was
actually a feel good story

00:11:23.227 --> 00:11:26.960
a once great American company,
having its second act.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.300
There's a ton of media attention
on it, everybody's asking

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:32.890
why this company, can Kodak really do it?

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:34.800
Why are there all these
strange things about

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.290
how they handled the announcement?

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:38.890
And then suddenly you have
everybody investigating.

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:42.540
Regulators look into
improprieties allegations

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:44.051
of insider trading.

00:11:44.051 --> 00:11:46.410
[MSNBC News Announcer] The
trading volume for Kodak

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:48.880
shot up right before the deal.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:51.990
Kodak, after the announcement
comes out, files that

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:54.440
just before the announcement
came out, they had

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:57.500
granted options to a
bunch of their executives.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:00.490
Which would seem to give
them access, the ability

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:03.270
to buy shares at a low price.

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:06.400
Dated to before the share
price really surged.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:08.870
Kodak said it made the
mistake of not placing

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:10.800
an embargo on the press release.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:13.560
And without the embargo,
journalists were quick to report

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.663
the news which impacted trading.

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:20.190
The source of the loan was
also a big area of interest.

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:21.023
It was given out by

00:12:21.023 --> 00:12:24.100
the U.S. International
Development Finance Corporation

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:25.760
or the DFC.

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.340
This is bout investing in
a company that is going to

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:31.470
not only produce 25% of the
generic pharmaceuticals that we

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:33.380
need the ingredients in the United States.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.310
But it's going to be
financially sustainable

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:36.143
for the long-term.

00:12:36.143 --> 00:12:38.930
So they had a big advanced
purchase order and we were able

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:42.860
to give them that money, the
$765 million collateralized

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:43.693
on that.

00:12:43.693 --> 00:12:46.580
So I knew we're gonna build
a brand, we're going to make

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:48.880
Americans safer and
we're going to make money

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.140
for the American taxpayer.

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:53.810
Randomly Adam Boehler is Jared Kushner,

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:57.800
Trump's son-in-law's
former summer roommate.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:12:59.720
So it's very Trump world.

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:03.220
So you have the DFC it's
this outward facing body

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:06.600
focused on international
development but it seemed like

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.670
you know, a friendly
entity in Trump world.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:11.630
While a review commissioned by Kodak says

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.870
CEO Jim Continenza's stock
options did not violate

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:19.340
internal policies, the SCC
investigation is ongoing.

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:23.020
And the damage in the eyes of
some has already been done.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:27.580
What happened at Kodak was
probably the dumbest decisions

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:30.780
made by executives in corporate history.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:33.070
Peter Navarro certainly
has plenty of reason to try

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:34.810
and distance himself from this deal.

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.150
You know, when he was announcing
it he was trying to take

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:41.110
all the credit in the world
and this was really his baby

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:43.440
trying to shepherd it through
and encouraging the government

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:45.033
to give Kodak this loan.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:49.380
The underlying question here
is, was Kodak some sort of

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:52.700
pump and dump scam, where by
shooting the share price up

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:56.358
with this loan announcement did
Kodak or anyone around Kodak

00:13:56.358 --> 00:14:01.358
unfairly profit off just the
share price skyrocketing alone?

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:04.080
In a statement Kodak said based on

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:07.240
an exhaustive review of
law and facts, our lawyers

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:10.510
have concluded that Kodak
and its officers, directors

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:12.960
and senior management did not violate

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.900
the securities regulations
or other relevant laws,

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:18.810
engage in a breach of
fiduciary duty or violate

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:21.883
any of Kodak's internal
policies and procedures.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:29.010
So while Kodak is in trouble,
both as a company and as

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:32.700
a company defining its identity,
it doesn't necessarily mean

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:34.720
that they made the wrong decisions.

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:38.030
Some say that companies just
aren't always meant to be

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:39.000
on top.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.380
It's not obvious that
companies exist forever.

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:45.270
The notion that a company
has to exist and if

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:48.210
it doesn't exist, somebody
failed, not accurate.

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:51.190
The function of management
is not to keep the company

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:54.360
in business, it's to create wealth.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:56.740
If you don't have a balance
where the revenue exceeds

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:59.372
your cost, you eventually
go out of business.

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:08.600
This isn't just an iconic
company, this isn't just

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:10.150
a corporate tragedy.

00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:13.493
It's a piece of America that's
being lost you know, forever.

